half progress to full thickness macular holes. In a patient with a macular hole in one eye, the risk of development of a
macular hole in the fellow eye is less than 2% if posterior vitreous detachment is present. If the posterior vitreous is
attached, the risk is approximately 15%. If an impending hole is found in the other eye, the risk rises to 50%.         
TREATMENT AND OUTCOMES Macular hole surgery is able to close full thickness macular holes in approximately 90% of cases.
